  "body": {
    "id": "prop:summary-closure-compact",
    "kind": "proposition",
    "statement": "The admissible summary image \\(\\mathscr S=S(\\mathcal M)\\) is uniformly bounded under \\(d_S\\). Hence its closure \\(\\mathcal K=\\overline{\\mathscr S}\\) is a compact subset of the finite-dimensional summary space. Moreover, \\(\\mathcal K\\) is nonempty if and only if \\(\\mathcal M\\) is nonempty.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "\\(\\mathscr S\\)",
      "\\(S(P)\\)",
      "\\(\\mathcal M\\)",
      "\\(d_S\\)",
      "\\(\\mathcal K\\)"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:bounded-x",
      "ass:bounded-proxy-product",
      "ass:bounded-outcome-proxy-product",
      "def:model-class",
      "def:observable-summary",
      "def:summary-closure",
      "prop:observed-vmw-margin-inclusion"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "For every \\(P\\in\\mathcal M\\), (1) of \\(\\mathrm{prop:observed\\text{-}vmw\\text{-}margin\\text{-}inclusion}\\) permits armwise conditioning, while its observable bounds give\n\\[\n\\lVert M_t(P)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}},\\ \\lVert N_t(P)\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}},\\ \n\\lVert m_X(P)\\rVert_2\\le L.                                                    \\tag{15}\n\\]\nThus \\(\\mathscr S=S(\\mathcal M)\\) lies in the product of four finite-dimensional operator-norm balls of radius \\(L\\) and one Euclidean ball of radius \\(L\\), and is bounded under \\(d_S\\).  Its closure \\(\\mathcal K\\) is closed and bounded.  To verify compactness without importing an undeclared result, take any sequence in \\(\\mathcal K\\).  Every scalar coordinate is bounded by norm equivalence in the fixed finite-dimensional summary space.  Successively extract convergent subsequences for the finitely many coordinates and take the final diagonal subsequence.  Its coordinatewise limit lies in \\(\\mathcal K\\) because \\(\\mathcal K\\) is closed, and coordinatewise convergence is \\(d_S\\)-convergence by finite-dimensional norm equivalence.  Hence \\(\\mathcal K\\) is sequentially compact and therefore compact in this metric space.  Finally, a closure is empty exactly when the original set is empty, and \\(S(\\mathcal M)=\\varnothing\\) exactly when \\(\\mathcal M=\\varnothing\\).",
    "justification": "Compactness makes nearest-summary projection exist whenever the statistical model is nonempty, while the explicit empty-class fallback makes the estimator total for incompatible constants.",
    "gap": "VMW works with admissible population moments but does not formulate the compact summary closure or the empty-class edge case needed for a total repair through collision.",
    "consumer": "The compact domain supports measurable nearest-point selection and the corrected confidence-set construction.",
